          5    Systems of polynomial equations for (9, 24) and (9, 27)
In this section we will prove the following theorem.
Theorem 5.1. There exist no pair of polynomials P, Q ∈ K[x, y] such that
   (1) [P, Q] = x + g(y) for some g(y) ∈ K[y],
   (2) en3,−1 (P ) = st1,0 (P ) = 2(3, 8) = (6, 16) and st−1,1 (P ) = en1,0 (P ) = 2(3, 9) = (6, 18),
   (3) en3,−1 (Q) = st1,0 (Q) = 3(3, 8) = (9, 24) and st−1,1 (Q) = en1,0 (Q) = 3(3, 9) = (9, 27),
    So we assume that such a pair exists, and we will arrive at a contradiction. By [1, Propo-
sitions 1.13 and 2.1] there exists a (1, 0)-homogeneous R with ℓ1,0 (P ) = R2 and ℓ1,0 (Q) = R3 .
Necessarily R = x3 C3 with C3 = y 8 (a0 + a1 y) for some a0 , a1 ∈ K × , and by a linear change of
variables we may also assume that C3 = y 8 (y + 1).
